  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1217 AM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nIsolated convection continues to bubble up along a cold front \nsinking south across the region. This activity should be south of \nour area by sunrise and any daytime convection on Thursday should \nremain south down in TN. Deep layer shear is weak (generally 20 kts \nat best) but we do still have DCAPE of 1000-1200 j/kg so can't rule \nout a few stronger storms capable of producing some decent wind over \nthe next several hours.\n\nUpper level troughing will reside across the Ohio Valley the next \ncouple of days resulting in a slightly cooler and less humid airmass \nthrough Saturday. The coldest night looks to be Friday night, when a \nfew stations may drop into the 50s again. As we head into the \nweekend, the upper ridge that has been parked over the southwest \nCONUS will shift eastward. As 850mb temps rise above 20C, we will \nsee highs back into the 90s by Sunday. Humidity levels remain low so \nheat index readings won't be much higher than actual temperatures \nthis weekend.\n\nGuidance is in good agreement keeping the 594dm ridge centered over \nthe OH/TN valley early to mid next week. While there is some \nuncertainty later in the week, the trend has been to keep the ridge \nin tact right through the end of the week. Some solutions even keep \nit in place through Labor Day weekend. It's quite possible this may \nturn into one of our longest heatwaves of the summer season.\n\nLREF probabilities for heat index readings exceeding 100 degrees are \n60-70% for Wed-Fri next week. Probabilities for MaxTs reaching at \nleast 95 degrees peak in the 70-80% range Tues-Fri. Given the time \nof year (waning influence of crops influencing dew points), the \nhumidity levels will be quite a bit lower than previous heatwaves \nthis summer. LREF only has about a 40-50% chance for dewpoints \nexceeding 70 degrees (a far cry from the upper 70s to low 80s we \nhave endured quite a bit this summer). Regardless though, heat \nimpacts are likely as MaxTs will be right up there near the highest \nwe have experienced this summer. Given the drier airmass in place, \nambient temps may end up hitting the upper 90s a couple of days and \nit's quite possible a few locations touch 100. Overnight lows also \nlook quite warm, with the LREF only suggesting a 20-40% chance \nreadings will drop below 75 degrees overnight Wed-Fri next week. \nThis may challenge some daily record warm lows, which are mostly in \nthe mid 70s in early September. HeatRisk also ramps up to major \ncategory by Tuesday and extreme on Wednesday, suggesting heat \nheadlines will likely be needed (particularly for the duration \naspect).",
